    meditation: `Augustine, halfway through the Confessions, walks into his own memory the way a steward walks into a great house and finds the rooms full. He calls them, in the Latin, campi et lata praetoria — the great fields and spacious palaces. The metaphor matters. Memory is not a closet. It is a country. There are corridors in it you have not entered in years. There are people standing in the long galleries whose names you would not remember if you passed them on the street, but who live in you still, and rise up when called.

The week opens with Augustine because no Christian writer has thought harder about what it means that a human being carries a country inside him. The tenth book of the Confessions is one of the founding texts of Western interiority, and it is also, if you read it as a lawyer, one of the founding texts of a doctrine of records. The mind, Augustine says, stores up everything the senses have brought in, and whatever the mind has added to those impressions by enlarging or diminishing them, and whatever it has done with them in dreams, and whatever else has not yet been swallowed and buried by forgetfulness. The categories are not so different from the categories of a discovery production. There are the originals. There are the derivatives. There are the documents you can find when you look. There are the documents that rise up unbidden when you were looking for something else.

You know this. You have searched for a file and turned up another. You have run a conflicts check on a new matter and watched the screen return a name you have not thought about in twelve years — a deponent, a paralegal, a co-defendant — and the room from which the deposition was taken rises up around you. The Augustinian point is that the rising is not a malfunction. It is the nature of the country you carry. Memory does what it does whether you ask it to or not, and part of the discipline of an interior life is learning what to do with what rises.

Augustine notices the other thing the lawyer notices: that memory has a will of its own. When I am in this storehouse, I ask that it be brought forth which I would; and some things come out presently, some require to be longer sought after, and are, as it were, fetched out from some inner receptacle. He is describing the experience of a man rummaging for the right name during direct examination. You ask, and the answer comes; or you ask, and the answer hides; or you ask for one thing and a troop of other things come rushing out instead, each one offering itself — Is it perchance I? You wave them off with what Augustine calls the hand of the heart, and you keep searching for the name you actually need.

This is a holy faculty. The pagan rhetoricians had treated memory as a craft, the fourth canon of rhetoric, a thing one trained with method-of-loci exercises and palace-of-memory diagrams. Augustine takes the same vocabulary and turns it inward. The palace is real, he says, but it is the palace God made when he made you. The capacity to hold a country inside yourself is a gift from the one who made the country. Memory is theological before it is rhetorical. To remember is to participate, in your small way, in the act by which God holds all that is in being.

For the lawyer this should reframe what your records are. The matter file is a paper extension of the palace. The cabinet behind your desk, the server in the back room, the closed-matter boxes piled in the storage unit you have not visited since 2017 — these are not bureaucratic apparatus. They are the institutional form of memory, and they share a grammar with the interior memory Augustine is exploring. The same questions arise. What did we keep? What did we lose? What rises up unbidden when we look for something else? What troops out at us, offering to be the answer to a question we did not ask?

There is also Augustine's quieter observation: forgetfulness has not yet swallowed up and buried what is in the palace. The not yet matters. He knows that forgetfulness will swallow most of it eventually. The great field is also a field that goes to seed. The corridors empty. The names of the deponents do, in fact, become unreachable, even when you run the conflicts check. The Christian thinker is not a hoarder. He knows the field is mortal, and he knows that what is kept is kept by a memory larger than his own. The eternal years, the psalm says. God holds the names you have lost. The closed matters are not closed to him.

This week sits inside that promise. Seven days on memory — Augustine, the cabinet, the lost cause, the client who keeps returning, the mentor gone, the law's memory, the society's annual reckoning. The arc is not nostalgic. Memory in this register is a virtue: it is the part of you that refuses to let what happened slip into nothing, because it knows that what happened is held by Someone, and that your holding is a small image of his.

Begin the week, then, by acknowledging the country. Sit at your desk for a moment before you open the first file. Notice the things in the room that are mnemonic. The diploma on the wall, with the names of professors who taught you. The photograph of the swearing-in. The Rolodex you have not used in a decade but did not throw out. The chipped coffee mug from a CLE in 2003. Each one is a marker in the great field, and each one is doing its quiet office: keeping for you what you would otherwise lose. Then open the file. The work today is being done by a man with a country inside him. Let the country be at his back as he writes.`,
