    meditation: `Day one hundred. A number that wants to mean something. It does not, of course; the calendar is arbitrary; but a lawyer who has been at this work long enough has learned that arbitrary numbers can be invitations to look around. Look at the room. Look, in particular, at what is in the corner.

In every lawyer's office there is a cabinet. Sometimes it is a file cabinet, the old steel kind, four drawers with the brass label-plates worn pale. Sometimes it is a closet full of bankers' boxes, the kind with the date scrawled on the side in marker. Sometimes it is a server, or a network share, or a cloud folder named CLOSED. In a sole practice it is all of these at once, and the boxes are the visible part. The matters have closed; the engagement letters have been archived; the bills have been sent and most of them paid; the clients have moved on; and the cabinet has remained.

What is in it. A divorce from 2012 in which the wife got the house and the husband got the boat, and you have heard from neither of them since. A wrongful-termination matter from 2009 that settled for less than it should have because the plaintiff was exhausted. A landlord-tenant case in which you represented the landlord and lost on a habitability defense you should have anticipated. A pro bono asylum case from a year when you were doing more pro bono and your billable hours suffered for it. Three or four estates whose probate is closed but whose files you cannot bring yourself to shred because you knew the families. A criminal appeal that was denied. A guardianship that turned ugly.

The cabinet is not nothing. It is the institutional form of your memory. Every box is a marker for a stretch of months of your life. The matter file is the official record, but it is also the unofficial record of the version of you who handled it. You can open any one of these boxes and recover not just the dispositive motion and the trial transcript but the smell of the conference room in which the depositions were taken, the sandwich shop where you ate during the recess, the parking garage you walked through afterwards in the rain. Discovery for the human being who handled the matter, not for the matter itself.

Cicero, in De Oratore, told the young Roman advocates that memory was the treasury and guardian of all things. He meant it about the speech you were trying to deliver without notes. We can let it mean more. The treasury of an advocate's working life is not the matters he is presently handling, which by their nature occupy the front of the desk and the front of the mind. The treasury is the matters he has handled — the closed work, the resolved disputes, the dispositions long since entered. That is what the cabinet keeps.

It keeps what the parties have stopped keeping. This is the strange office of the lawyer's records. Your client has, almost certainly, thrown away her copy of the file. The opposing party has done the same. The court's docket has been moved to archival storage and is searchable only by case number. The witnesses have gone on with their lives and would, if pressed, give you a different account of what happened than the transcript records. The only complete contemporaneous record of the matter that still exists in any organized form is yours. You are the keeper. You did not volunteer for this office; you took it on by accepting the engagement. But it is yours.

There is a Catholic instinct to honor this. The Church has always insisted that records matter — sacramental records, especially, the baptismal registers and the marriage registers, kept in parish safes for centuries. The Council of Trent codified the requirement: baptisms, confirmations, marriages, the deaths of the faithful, all to be set down in a hand that would outlast the hand. The local parish became, almost incidentally, the institutional memory of the village. When the genealogist comes looking for the great-great-grandmother, it is the parish record that yields her name, kept by priests who had no idea anyone would ever ask. The Christian tradition is that we keep, faithfully, what we have been given to keep, even when we cannot see why.

The cabinet in the corner has the same quiet dignity. Your closed matters are not stored because you might one day need them again, though occasionally you do. They are stored because you were entrusted with a piece of someone's life, and the trust does not end on the day the case ends. It continues, in the lesser form of careful keeping, for as long as the file is preserved. The client may never need her file again. She may never think of you again. The fact that her file is still in your cabinet, organized, retrievable, undamaged, is a small fidelity that does not require her to know about it to be real.

There is also a more sober reading. Some of those boxes contain matters you handled badly. The settlement you should not have recommended. The brief in which you missed an argument. The client you patronized without quite realizing it until much later. The cabinet keeps that too. The temptation is to burn it, in the metaphorical sense — to forget, to file mentally under closed and done, to never reopen the box. Augustine would say this is a false peace. The forgetting that swallows what we have done is not the same as the absolution that lifts it. The honest lawyer leaves the box where it is, and when, in the course of a conflicts check or a random retrieval, the name comes back, he acknowledges what he did or failed to do, asks forgiveness if needed, and sets the box back down. The keeping is itself a kind of penance.

Go, today, and look at the cabinet. Not for any practical reason. Open one drawer. Read the labels. Try to remember, in five minutes, the name of one client whose file is in that drawer and what happened in the matter. You may succeed; you may not. Either way, the cabinet has been doing its quiet work for you, holding what you could not hold by yourself. Acknowledge that it has done so. Then close the drawer, and go back to the matters of the day, which will, in their turn, become matters of the cabinet.`,
