    meditation: `There is a case you remember that the other side has forgotten. They won. They went on. The corporation closed the matter, the in-house lawyers moved to other files, the partner who tried it against you retired, the witnesses dispersed, the appellate ruling was unpublished and is no longer cited. As a matter in the world, the case is finished. It exists, now, only in two places: in your memory, and in God's.

This is a particular grief of the advocate, and it is one of the reasons a week on memory could not be honestly written without sitting with it. You lost something that mattered. You lost it not because you were lazy or unprepared; you lost it because the evidence was insufficient, or the bench was hostile, or the law as it stood did not let your client win, or any of the other things that go wrong in matters that deserve to go right. The client who needed the win did not get it. She may have moved on too, with whatever was left of her life. The case was a chapter for her. It was a chapter for you also, but a different kind of chapter, one you keep rereading.

The winners do not remember. This is part of the geometry of victory. To win is to be permitted to forget, because the matter is closed in your favor and there is nothing to revisit. The Ten Commandments are full of God commanding his people to remember — remember the Sabbath, remember the deliverance from Egypt, remember the manna — because the children of Israel had been on the losing end of Egypt and were now on the winning end of Sinai, and the temptation of winners is exactly to forget. Memory is, in scripture, very often a discipline of the redeemed. The losers, of course, do not need the discipline. They remember because they cannot help it.

Ambrose, writing his De Officiis in late-fourth-century Milan and very consciously writing it against Cicero's pagan original, included a passage on the labor of the just man for a cause that cannot be won. It is one of the more pastoral things in the book. He had been a Roman governor before he was a bishop; he knew what it was to lose a case. His insight is not that the loss is secretly a win — that would be cheap. His insight is that the plea was real, the pleading is recorded somewhere, and the record is not held by the human court whose ruling went against you. There is a higher court, he says, where no good word falls to the ground. The brief you wrote in 2006 for the client who lost is still on file in that court. It has not been archived. It is not gathering dust. It is alive.

This is not consolation prize theology. Ambrose is not saying that the loss did not matter. The loss mattered; the client lost what she lost; you cannot pretend that the harm did not happen. What he is saying is that the moral substance of the work — the laboring for the cause — is conserved, the way energy is conserved in physics. It does not disappear. It is taken up into the larger economy of intention and perseverance that God is weighing. You have not been pleading into the void all these years. You have been pleading into a record that is being kept by someone with infinite patience.

A practical question follows. What do you do with the memory of the lost cause? There are several wrong things you can do. You can repress it, file it mentally under failures, and refuse to revisit. This works for a few years and then fails; the case will come back when you do not want it to, in the middle of a different deposition, while you are driving the children to school, at three in the morning. You can also indulge it — turn the lost case into a private monument, narrate it to yourself with increasing self-pity, let it become the justification for not taking the next hard case because the last one did not work out. This too fails; it ages badly; it produces the embittered advocate everyone has met at the bar association dinner.

The right thing is harder and quieter. It is to remember the case the way a Christian remembers any suffering: as something that has not yet been redeemed in time but has already been received into the redemptive economy. The case is held. The client is held. The loss is held. You can take it up, look at it, name what went wrong, name what you would do differently, and set it back down. The not-yet-being-redeemed-in-time part of it is real. There may not be a satisfactory resolution to that loss this side of the grave. The point is not to fabricate one. The point is to remember the case with hope, which is a different thing from remembering it with optimism.

There are other people in this with you. Every lawyer who has practiced more than a few years has a lost case in the back of her mind. The eviction that should have been defensible. The asylum case in which the country conditions changed at exactly the wrong moment. The criminal trial in which the cooperator's testimony was just barely enough. The civil rights case in which the qualified immunity doctrine swallowed the substance. Talk about these cases with other lawyers. Not the war-story version, the embellished one. The actual version. The version in which you say, this is the one I still think about, and I do not know what to do with it. The unburdening is itself a small act of memory — a sharing of the load that, in the older Christian sense, is what fellowship was for.

There is, finally, the figure on the cross. The Just One himself had a cause that, in the court of his day, was lost. He pleaded and was condemned. He has, in his risen body, the wounds of that loss. They are not erased. They are kept. The doctrine of the resurrection is not the doctrine of forgetting the crucifixion; it is the doctrine of carrying its marks into the throne room. The lawyer who has lost cases stands closer to that figure than the lawyer who has not. Hold your lost cause beside his. Let your unhealed go beside his never-erased. Then go back to work.`,
