    meditation: `There is a category of client whose matter closed years ago and who has nevertheless not left your head. The case file is in the cabinet; the engagement letter is no longer in effect; the woman, or the man, or the family, is presumably going about life elsewhere; and yet, in moments you do not control, they come back. The asylum client whose date of birth you remember without looking it up. The custody client whose children, by now, are nearly grown. The probate client whose dead husband you somehow can still picture, though you only met him through photographs the widow brought to the consultation.

This is one of the strangest costs and gifts of the work. Most professions do not produce this experience. The accountant does not, generally, have his former clients walk through his head at random in the grocery store. The architect, perhaps, when he drives past one of his buildings. The doctor, sometimes, when a patient he has treated dies and the death notice runs in the paper. But the lawyer, especially the lawyer who has done family work, immigration work, criminal defense work, plaintiffs' work — the lawyer who has been involved in the deep crises of strangers' lives — accumulates these returning presences in a way that is hard to explain to people outside the profession.

What do you do with them. The conventional wisdom, especially in the wellness literature aimed at lawyers, is that you should not carry them. Boundaries. Self-care. The matter is closed; the relationship is over; let it go. There is some truth in this, especially for the lawyer who has been bleeding into every case and not sleeping. But there is also something un-Christian about the formulation, and it is worth naming what.

The Christian tradition does not say that the persons we have loved or served drop out of our lives when the active relationship ends. It says, on the contrary, that there is a deeper structure called the communion of saints, in which persons remain in one another's prayers, in one another's intercessions, in one another's offered actions, after every visible tie has been dissolved. Newman, in the sermon excerpted today, takes this further than the modern reader expects. The communion includes not only the canonized; it includes the ordinary dead and the ordinary living we have known only in passing. The ties of which we are scarcely conscious. The duty performed for them does not end when the door closes.

Apply this to the woman whose immigration case you handled in 2014. She came in; she sat across from you; she told you her story; the matter went well, or badly, or in between; she left. She is now living a life you know nothing about. But the work you did for her, and the time you spent listening to her, did something to both of you. There was an exchange. Some part of her is in you because she sat in your office for six hours over the course of three meetings; some part of you, however small, is in her because she trusted a stranger with documents she had carried across a border. The relationship is closed in the legal sense and is not closed in the human sense. Newman's intuition is that this open thread is not a malfunction. It is one of the ways God knits human beings together over time.

Paul, in the opening of his letter to the Philippians, names this directly. I give thanks to my God in every remembrance of you. He is writing to a church he founded and has not seen in some time. He could have written a businesslike letter; he writes instead a letter that begins with thanksgiving for memory itself. The remembrance is good. The remembrance is occasion for prayer. The persons he has served and who have served him are not lost to him; they are present to him in the strange immediate way that memory presents people. And he turns this presence into intercession. He prays for them, in joy.

This is what to do with the clients who keep returning. Pray for them. The discipline is small and quiet. The woman from 2014 walks into your head while you are washing the dishes. You do not need to figure out why she came. You do not need to remember every detail of the matter. You can say, in the moment, Lord, be with her wherever she is tonight, and finish the dishes. The intrusion has been converted into intercession. The returning has been received as an invitation.

This is also one of the small ways your life as a lawyer becomes inseparable from your life as a Christian. The pagan version of the returning client is haunting: the case is over, the woman is gone, why is she still here, this is what therapy is for. The Christian version is communion: the case is over, the woman has moved on with her visible life, and we are nevertheless linked, by ties we are scarcely conscious of, and the link is not a wound but a vocation. She came into your life because someone needed someone at her side, and the someone-at-her-side does not stop being someone-at-her-side just because the matter file has been closed and stored.

There are limits to this. You are not, in any continuing professional sense, her lawyer. You may not contact her. You may not insert yourself into her later life because you remember her. The intercession is interior, and largely silent, and that is exactly its character. It is not surveillance. It is the prayer of a man who once did a piece of work for a person and continues, decades later, to bring her quietly before God when she comes to mind, and to thank God that she crossed his path at all.

Make a small list, then, this week. Five clients, no more. Five matters that, when you ask, return. Do not try to recover all the details; details are not the point. Take the list to Mass on Sunday, or to the kitchen table where you pray in the morning, and say each name in turn. Say a short prayer for each. You will not be able to maintain this as an everyday discipline; that is not the point either. The point is to recognize, once, that the persons you have served are not gone from you, and that this is not a burden, but the shape of the communion you stand inside.`,
