    meditation: `There comes a day in every long career when you put on the dark suit, drive to a funeral home in a part of the city you have not been in for years, and pay respects to a lawyer who shaped you. The widow stands by the door. There are other lawyers in the receiving line, some of whom you have not seen since trial in a courthouse that has since been renovated. The casket is at the front of the room. A picture of the lawyer in his prime — younger than you would have ever known him — sits on an easel. The room smells of carnations and the carpet cleaner of the funeral home, and you stand in a line, and you do not, at first, know what to say.

What you eventually say is a story. The mentor was good at depositions, or he was relentless on cross, or he gave a closing once in front of Judge so-and-so that you have never forgotten. You tell the story to his daughter, who did not see him in trial. She has heard a hundred stories like this today, and each one has been a piece of her father given back to her by a stranger. The funeral becomes, over an hour, a kind of distributed reconstitution of a man. Not the man as he was at home — that part she knows — but the man as he was in the work, which she only knew secondhand, and which is now being returned to her in fragments by people who saw him in the rooms she could not enter.

This is one of the deep functions of a mentor's funeral, and it is one of the deep occasions for the lawyer's memory to do its work. Most professions do not gather this way. A doctor's funeral is attended by family, friends, sometimes a few colleagues. A lawyer's funeral, especially a trial lawyer's, is attended by an entire generation of people whose lives he shaped, half of whom are themselves now mentors of younger lawyers who never met him. The room becomes a small council of the bar — adversaries, co-counsel, clerks who became partners, opposing counsel he beat, opposing counsel who beat him. The mentor's life is told and retold in a hundred small narrations across the room, and each narration adds something to the next one.

The Christian instinct is to honor this. Ecclesiasticus 44 — Let us now praise men of renown, and our fathers in their generation — is one of the great passages of biblical praise of the dead. It is, when you sit with it, almost a Christian doctrine of professional formation. The writer is naming the fathers who built the people of God: the founders, the kings, the prophets, the wise. He praises not their personal sanctity in the abstract but the work they did and the line of inheritance they established. They that were born of them have left a name behind them, that their praises might be related. The naming and the praising are not flattery. They are how a tradition stays alive across generations.

Newman, who outlived most of his Oxford friends and most of his Birmingham colleagues, wrote often about what it means to lose someone you have looked up to. The passage excerpted today catches the texture exactly. There is a stillness in the place he occupied which no other voice will fill in just that way. You know this. The phone call you used to make to the mentor when you had a hard ethics question — you no longer make it. The chair he sat in at the bar association lunch is filled by someone else now. The trial style he taught you is being practiced by you in a courthouse he never set foot in. He is gone from the visible profession, and the absence is not generic; it has a particular shape, the shape of him.

And yet, as Newman insists, what was given is given still. The cross-examination technique he showed you in 1997 is now the technique you are quietly teaching the third-year associate who started in your firm last fall. She does not know whose technique it is. You may or may not mention his name. Either way, the line of teaching is not broken. The hand that hands it on has, for a moment, the warmth of the hand that gave it. The mentor lives in you in the small physical ways that mentors actually live in apprentices: the way you hold a yellow pad when listening, the way you pause before answering a hostile question, the way you write a thank-you note to a deposition court reporter.

This is the communion of saints in its professional inflection. The Catholic doctrine of the communion does not stop at the church door. It stretches into the workshop, the courtroom, the operating theater, the studio, the schoolroom. Wherever a craft is handed down, there is a small visible image of the larger handing-down by which the gospel itself comes to each generation. Your mentor is one of the saints of your trade, in the lowercase and sometimes the uppercase sense. He is praying for you, the Catholic tradition would say, more freely now than he could when he was your colleague and rival.

There are practical things to do with the memory of a mentor gone. Tell his stories. Tell them well, with specificity, without exaggeration. Tell them to the younger lawyers, and tell them to your own children if you have them, and tell them at the bar association memorial when you are asked. Write the story down if you have the discipline. Most lawyers do not write their mentors down, and the mentors fade in a generation. There is a small literature of memoirs by lawyers, and it is not as good as the literature of memoirs by writers, but it is better than nothing, and the man who shaped you may deserve the few hours it would take to put one story in writing while you still remember the details.

Go also, sometimes, to the grave. This is a Catholic practice that the wider culture has lost and that the lawyer can recover. Visit the cemetery. Stand for a minute. Say a Hail Mary, or whatever is in your tradition. Then go back to the work, which is his work too, still being done, still being handed on.`,
    prayer:
      "Lord of the eternal years, you took my mentor home, and the stillness he has left I cannot fill alone. What he gave me, let me give faithfully to others. Hold him in your peace; hold his line of teaching in mine; let me, when my own students gather at my funeral, have something worth handing on. Amen.",
    prompt:
      "Name a mentor who shaped your practice. What of his is in your hands today, and to whom are you passing it on?",
    situationalTags: ["death-of-mentor", "gratitude-for-the-bar"],
