    meditation: `There is a strange asymmetry between what the law remembers and what we remember. The law has built into itself a doctrine of forgetting: the statute of limitations. A claim that is not brought within a certain number of years is barred. The wrong is no longer cognizable. The court will not hear the case. The plaintiff may know in his bones that the harm happened; the defendant may know in his bones that he did it; neither knowing matters anymore, because the law has decided that finality is a higher value than continued litigation of old grievances.

This is, in its way, one of the wisest doctrines the common law has built. Without it, every dispute would be eternal. A grandfather's debt could be sued upon by a great-grandson. A wrong done in childhood could be litigated in old age, with witnesses dead and documents lost. Society could not function under perpetual liability. The statute of limitations is the law's institutional act of saying, at some point, this is over. The matter is closed not because it was rightly resolved but because we cannot let it go on.

It is also the law's institutional act of saying that human memory degrades. Witnesses forget. Documents are lost. The longer the gap, the less reliable any factual reconstruction will be. The statute is a concession to mortality. By the time twenty years have passed, the truth of what happened is no longer recoverable in the way a court requires it to be recoverable. The law, in setting a number, is acknowledging what Augustine acknowledged in the tenth book of the Confessions: forgetfulness swallows up and buries. Eventually the field is gone.

But the lawyer who has been at this for any length of time knows the other side of this doctrine. The law's statute of limitations is not the same as the human heart's. The harm that the law no longer recognizes is still felt by the person to whom it was done. The wrong that the law has declared barred is not actually barred in the moral universe; it is only barred in the procedural one. The widow whose husband was killed at work in 1985, in a way that turned out, decades later, to be the company's fault — the law tells her she cannot sue. It does not tell her she cannot grieve. It does not tell her that her husband is now to be forgotten because the limitations period has run.

This is one of the more painful explanations a lawyer has to give at intake. The client comes in. She has just learned something. The thing happened twenty-five years ago and was hidden from her, and now it is uncovered, and she wants to act on it. You sit with her. You explain the doctrine. You explain that the law's interest in finality is, in this case, the wall standing between her and any remedy in court. She does not, often, understand at first. When she does understand, she does not, often, accept. She thinks the law has done her a second wrong on top of the first. You cannot fully refute her. You can only stand in the difficulty of being the messenger.

There is a deeper Christian word in this. Aquinas, in the secunda secundae, treats memory as a part of prudence. The prudent man, he says, is the one who learns from the past in order to act well in the future. But the memory that contributes to prudence must be true — conformable to what actually was, not corrupted by the affections. The man who remembers what he wishes were true rather than what is has destroyed the instrument of his own prudence. This is the great challenge of the long memory. Over time, our memories edit themselves. We become the heroes of our own remembered conflicts. The injuries we did become smaller; the injuries done to us become larger. Memory drifts, and prudence drifts with it.

The law's statute of limitations is, in part, a response to this drift. It is the law's acknowledgement that, past a certain point, even a sincere memory cannot be trusted enough to underwrite a verdict. This is not cynicism. It is anthropology. The same person who would swear with perfect honesty in year three that the contract said X may, in year fifteen, swear with equal honesty that it said Y. The drift is not lying. It is the ordinary degradation of human memory. The law does what it can with this fact, and the statute is one of its tools.

Set beside this the doctrine of God's forgetting. Isaias, speaking for the Lord: I will not remember thy sins. The Hebrew is stronger than the English; it means actively to blot out, to refuse to bring to mind. There is a forgetting in God which is not a degradation of memory — God's memory does not degrade — but an act of mercy by which what has been forgiven is not held against the forgiven one. This is the deeper finality that the human statute of limitations dimly images. The law says, after a certain interval, we will not adjudicate this anymore. God says, of repented sin, I will not remember this anymore. Both are forms of letting go. Neither is forgetting in the colloquial sense; both are decisions, made by an authority with the right to make them, that the matter is closed.

The Christian lawyer is asked to hold both of these in one hand. The law's finality, which protects the social order; God's finality, which redeems the soul. They are not the same. The law's statute of limitations is a procedural rule that may close a wronged person out of remedy; it may serve justice, or it may obstruct it. The mercy of God is never obstruction. It is the deeper finality on which the lesser one rests.

When you next have to tell a client that her matter is time-barred, do not pretend the doctrine is morally satisfying. It is not always morally satisfying. Acknowledge with her that the harm was real and that the law's silence on the harm is itself a kind of silence to grieve. Then point, gently, past the doctrine to the harder and better question: what does she do with the memory of the wrong now that the courts will not act on it? The answer is not in your hands. It is in the hands of the one who blots out iniquities for his own sake, and who holds even what the law has barred. Pray for her on the way out. The matter that the law has closed is still open in the higher court.`,
