    meditation: `Today is Tax Day in the United States. The deadline by which the citizenry, by an act of administrative remembrance, declares to the federal government the year's income, the year's deductions, the year's obligations. It is a strange and revealing ritual. Once a year, an entire society sits down and writes out, in numbers, what it has earned and what it owes. The form is dry; the act, considered theologically, is not.

A tax return is a memory document. It says: this is what came in. This is what went out. This is what was given. This is what is owed. The taxpayer is required, on penalty of perjury, to remember accurately. The accountant or the lawyer who prepares the return is required, on penalty of professional discipline, to keep the memory clean. The government, in turn, is required to accept the return as the truthful record of the year and to act on it accordingly, subject to audit. The entire scheme rests on the assumption that a society can, once a year, perform a collective act of honest memory about itself.

This is the seventh and final day of a week on memory, and Tax Day is the right place to land. We began with Augustine's interior palace; we end with a civic act of communal remembering. The arc is intentional. Christian memory is not only individual; it is corporate. The Church, every Sunday, anamnesis — the Greek word for the remembering done in the Eucharist, the active calling-to-mind that makes a past act present. The state, every spring, has its own much smaller ritual: the annual collective remembering of what we owe to one another through the medium of the public fisc. The two acts are not on the same plane, but they share a grammar.

The Gospel passage is the famous one. They show him a coin. He asks whose image is on it. They answer, Caesar's. Render therefore to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's. The Lord is not saying that the coin and the soul belong to two unrelated empires. He is saying that each has its proper bearer of image, and each is therefore due to its proper sovereign. The coin bears Caesar's image. The man bears God's. Render each to whom it belongs.

Leo XIII, in Rerum Novarum, drew the practical conclusion that has shaped Catholic teaching on the state ever since. The members of civil society, by paying tribute, do that which is just; for they live by the order which the State preserves. This is not flattery of the tax collector. Leo is realistic, even sharp, about the limits of state authority elsewhere in the encyclical. But he insists that there is a real reciprocity between the citizen and the commonwealth. You live by an order. The order requires upkeep. The upkeep requires resources. The resources come from the citizens. To pay what is owed is not a grudging concession; it is a recognition of the order under which you have flourished.

For the lawyer this should reframe Tax Day. You probably do not enjoy April 15. You may be filing for an extension. You may be writing a check larger than you wanted to write. You may have spent the weekend gathering documents from a year you would rather not relive in such fiscal detail. None of that is wrong to feel. But the underlying ritual is doing work that is, in the broad Christian sense, good. The society is remembering itself. The roads, the courts, the public defenders, the schools, the army, the agencies — all of them are funded by an annual act of communal recollection, and you are a participant in that act.

The lawyer has a particular relationship to this. You may do tax work; many lawyers do, in some capacity, even if they are not tax lawyers in the technical sense. You have certainly given clients advice about what they owe. You have, in many cases, helped them remember more carefully than they would have remembered on their own. The deductible expense they would have forgotten. The 1099 income they had not tracked. The capital improvement on the rental property that should have been depreciated. Part of your work, considered theologically, is helping the citizenry remember itself accurately to the public order. This is not the most romantic version of legal practice. It is one of the most consequential.

The figure of the tax collector in the Gospels is, of course, also relevant. He is the despised figure of first-century Palestine: collaborator with Rome, extortionist of his own people, a man whom no Pharisee would touch. And Jesus eats with him. Matthew, who wrote the Gospel from which today's passage is taken, was himself a tax collector before he was called. Zacchaeus, the chief tax collector of Jericho, climbs the tree to see him and ends the encounter promising to pay back fourfold anything he has wrongly taken. The Gospel is not naive about tax collection; it knows the office is corruptible. But it refuses to treat the tax collector as outside redemption, and it refuses to treat the act of paying tribute as outside the moral life.

There is also the broader question of what a society's memory of obligation looks like. A society that cannot remember what it owes does not last long. It forgets the veterans, then the elderly, then the children, then the poor, then the infrastructure on which all of these depend. The annual ritual of the return is, among other things, a disciplinary practice against this forgetting. It compels the well-off to acknowledge, in numbers, that they are part of an order they did not create alone, and that the order has claims on them. The lawyer who has counseled wealthy clients knows how readily this is resisted. The resistance is, in part, a refusal of memory. The wealth came from somewhere. It came partly from the schools that trained the workforce, the courts that enforced the contracts, the police that protected the warehouse, the highways that moved the goods. Tax Day is the day the country says, gently or sharply, do not forget.

Close the week, then, with this small civic act of memory. If you are filing today, file. If you are paying, pay. Say a brief prayer for those who collect the tribute and for those who decide how it will be spent. Say a brief prayer of thanks for the order that has held you up this year. The tax return is dry on the surface; the act underneath it is a society remembering itself, and you are a participant. Memoria — what you keep — closes here, in the most ordinary of rooms, with a stamped envelope, a calculator, and a small willingness to render to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's.`,
